(references/queries-postgres.md); Mongo specifics: [`references/queries-mongo.md`](references/queries-mongo.md) |\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:75:      `@internal/mongo-lowering` adds `MongoQueryable`, with `execute` and `run`, as `SqlQueryable` is for SQL. `MongoDriver<TBinding = void>` extends it and adds `connect(binding)` and `close()`, as `SqlDriver<TBinding>` does. `MongoControlDriverInstance` extends `MongoQueryable`, not `MongoDriver`, so a control driver has no `connect`. `executeDdl`, `MongoRunnerDependencies.driver` and the migration runner now take `MongoQueryable`; `createMongoRuntime` takes `MongoDriver<unknown>`. Type code that only sends commands, or that receives a control driver, as `MongoQueryable`. A runtime driver you implement as `MongoDriver` adds `connect`. The detection matches classes that implement `MongoDriver`, interfaces and classes that extend it, type aliases that name it, and values, parameters and return types annotated or checked as `MongoDriver`, also inside a generic such as `Promise<MongoDriver>`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:108:      `MongoRuntimeOptions` is `{ context, adapter, driver, middleware?, mode? }`, as the SQL runtime's options are: the caller creates the adapter with `stack.adapter.create(stack)`, and the runtime checks middleware against `context.contract.target`, so `context` is a `MongoExecutionContext<MongoContract>`. `MongoExecutionContext` is declared in `@prisma/orm-family-mongo/query-ast/execution` (`@internal/mongo-query-ast/execution`), still exported from the runtime entry; it has no `stack` member and no second type parameter (`TTargetId`), and its type parameter is constrained as SQL's `ExecutionContext` is: `MongoExecutionContext<TContract extends MongoContract = MongoContract>`, and `createMongoExecutionContext` takes the same constraint. `createMongoExecutionStack()` still takes an optional `driver`; given one, it returns `MongoExecutionStackWithDriver`, whose `driver` is always set, and without one it returns `MongoExecutionStack`, which has no `driver` member, as `createSqlExecutionStack()` does. `buildMongoEnums()` and `MongoEnumAccessors` are removed, and `MongoExecutionContext` has no `enums` member, as SQL's `ExecutionContext` has none: the static context builds `db.enums` with the framework's `buildNamespacedEnums`, as `postgresStatic()` does, and the ORM builds the accessors it checks writes against from the context, once per client. The detection matches files that call `createMongoRuntime(`, read `.stack` from a context, give `MongoExecutionContext` two type arguments or a non-contract type argument, or name `buildMongoEnums` or `MongoEnumAccessors`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:133:      `connect()` without options on a `postgres()`, `sqlite()`, `mysql()` or `mongo()` client no longer rejects when the client is connecting or connected. It waits for the connection and resolves with the runtime. `connect(options)` on such a client still rejects with `DRIVER.ALREADY_CONNECTED`, but its message is now `<Target> client is already bound` (for example `Postgres client is already bound`), it has a `why`, and its `fix` changed. Update code or tests that branch on `DRIVER.ALREADY_CONNECTED` after a `connect()` without options, or that match the old message `<Target> client already connected` or the old `fix` \"Call connect() at most once per client.\". Code that avoided a second `connect()` for fear of this error may call it freely. A `connect()` that is waiting when `close()` is called now rejects with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:251:const driverInstance = clientStack.driver.create();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:274:Put the descriptor on the execution stack, create the driver from the stack, and connect it to a binding. A `{ kind: 'url', url, dbName }` binding makes the driver open a `MongoClient` and close it on `close()`. A `{ kind: 'mongoClient', client, dbName }` binding uses your client and leaves it open.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:291:const driver = stack.driver.create();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:295:Replace `MongoDriverImpl.fromDb(client.db(dbName))` with `driver.connect({ kind: 'mongoClient', client, dbName })` on a driver created the same way. Code that only sends DDL through a control adapter can use the control driver instead: `await mongoControlDriver.create(url)`, with `mongoControlDriver` the default export of `@prisma/orm-target-mongo/driver/control`. The control driver uses the database the URL names, so put `dbName` in the URL's path, for example `mongodb://localhost:27017/app`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:297:`createMongoRuntime(...)` closes the driver when the runtime closes, so keep calling `runtime.close()` and drop any separate `driver.close()`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:322:  async close(): Promise<void> {}\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:336:  async close(): Promise<void> {}\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:381:const runtime = createMongoRuntime({ context, adapter: stack.adapter.create(stack), driver });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.15-to-0.16/instructions.md:23:      `contract infer`'s back-relation field name generation used a hand-rolled pluralization rule that appended `es` to any table name already ending in `s`/`x`/`z`/`ch`/`sh`, doubling an already-plural table name (`sessions` -> `sessionses`). `contract infer` now uses real inflection (the `pluralize` library) and produces the correct name (`sessions` stays `sessions`; a genuinely singular `status` still becomes `statuses`). This only affects a future `contract infer` run — an already-generated `.prisma` file is untouched, so nothing breaks until you next re-run infer. If you do re-run `contract infer` against a database with an already-plural table name, diff the regenerated `.prisma` file for any back-relation field whose name changed — that's a public field name your application code accesses via `.include()`/`.select()`/the generated TypeScript types, so update those call sites to the corrected name.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/0.15-to-0.16/instructions.md:25:      `contract infer`'s back-relation field name generation used a hand-rolled pluralization rule that appended `es` to any table name already ending in `s`/`x`/`z`/`ch`/`sh`, doubling an already-plural table name (`sessions` -> `sessionses`). `contract infer` now uses real inflection (the `pluralize` library) and produces the correct name (`sessions` stays `sessions`; a genuinely singular `status` still becomes `statuses`). This only affects a future `contract infer` run — an already-generated `.prisma` file is untouched, so nothing breaks until you next re-run infer for your pack. If you do re-run `contract infer` against a schema with an already-plural table name, diff the regenerated `.prisma` file for any back-relation field whose name changed — that's a public field name your pack's consumers access via `.include()`/`.select()`/the generated TypeScript types, so a rename is a breaking change to your pack's own published surface, to be versioned and documented like any other.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:15:- User is running a one-off script (`tsx my-script.ts`, Node CLI, CI task) and the process won't exit after queries finish, or they need script teardown (`db.close()`, `await using`).\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:66:The Mongo façade has the same construction shape — `import mongo from '@internal/mongo/runtime'` — and the same `db.connect(...)` / `db.close()` lifecycle methods. **The Mongo façade does not expose `db.transaction(...)`.** See *What Prisma 8 doesn't do yet* for the workaround. **The ORM surface differs in one place: keys.** On Mongo, `db.orm` is keyed by the collection's storage name (from `@@map(...)`, or the lowercased model name if no `@@map` is set), not by the PSL model name — so `model User { … @@map(\"users\") }` is reached at `db.orm.users`, not `db.orm.public.User`. The SQL builder lane (`db.sql.<ns>.<table>`) doesn't exist on Mongo at all (`db.sql` is `undefined`). See `references/queries.md` § *MongoDB ORM addressing* for the full rule and a rewrite recipe for SQL-target examples.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:70:The concept: short scripts that connect, query, then expect the process to exit will **hang on Postgres** because the `pg.Pool` owned by `postgres()` keeps Node's event loop alive. The data round-trip succeeds; the script never exits. Call `await db.close()` before the script returns (or use `await using` **at the top of a script module** so teardown runs when the module exits — see the block-scope warning below for why this matters).\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:78:const created = await db.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com', name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:82:await db.close();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:85:**TS 5.2+ idiomatic shape** — construct the client at the **top of a script module** and let `[Symbol.asyncDispose]` call `close()` when the module exits:\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:96:// db.close() runs automatically when the script module exits.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:109:  const users = await db.orm.public.User.all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:124:  const users = await db.orm.public.User.all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:129:Servers (HTTP handlers, workers in a request loop) **do not call `db.close()`** at all in steady state. The pool stays open for the process lifetime. `db.close()` and `await using` are for short-lived scripts — `tsx my-script.ts`, Node CLI commands, CI tasks, one-off seed runs — not for code that runs inside a request loop.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:133:- **`close()` is idempotent.** Calling it twice is a no-op.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:134:- **`close()` is terminal.** There is no reconnect on a closed `db` — construct a new client if you need to reach the database again. After close, `db.connect(...)`, `db.query(...)`, `db.execute(...)`, `db.transaction(...)`, `db.prepare(...)` and ORM calls reject with `Error('<target> client is closed')` (e.g. `'Postgres client is closed'`, `'SQLite client is closed'`, `'Mongo client is closed'`); none of them throws at the call, so `.catch(...)` sees the error. A mistake in the call itself, such as an ORM query the database's capabilities do not allow, still throws at the call. `db.runtime()` returns a runtime, so on Postgres and SQLite it throws the same error at the call; on MongoDB it returns a promise, which rejects.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:135:- **`close()` waits for work already running and refuses new work.** On a Postgres client built from `{ url }`, `close()` refuses every new call at once with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Postgres client is closed\"), and a runtime captured with `db.runtime()` before `close()` is refused at once with \"Runtime is closed\"; it waits for the queries, transactions and ORM calls already in flight, then ends the pool. An ORM call that resolves to one value, such as `first()`, `create()` or a nested create with its reload, starts when it is called, so `close()` waits for it and it completes. On a serverless connection, `close()` waits until the runtime has been idle for one turn of the event loop, so a chain of queries that keeps it busy from the close onward completes, then ends the `pg.Client`; work that starts after that rejects with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Runtime is closed\"). A lazy result such as `db.orm...all()` or `db.query(plan)` starts when it is awaited or iterated, not when it is built; awaited after `close()`, an ORM `all()` is refused with \"Postgres client is closed\". A Postgres client given a caller's `pg.Pool` or `pg.Client` does not close its runtime, so work through a runtime taken before `close()` keeps running on the caller's pool. `await` outstanding work before calling `close()`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:136:- **Ownership.** `close()` releases only what the façade constructed (`pg.Pool` from `{ url }`, `MongoClient` from `{ url }` / `{ uri, dbName }`, SQLite handle from `{ path }`). If you supplied your own `pg.Pool` / `pg.Client` (Postgres `pg:` option), `mongodb.MongoClient` (Mongo `mongoClient:` option), or a pre-built `binding`, `db.close()` does **not** touch those — you own their lifecycle.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:138:**`db.end()` does not exist.** The universal `node-postgres` name is `pool.end()` on a `pg.Pool`; the Prisma 8 runtime client is not a `pg.Pool`. The right call is `await db.close()`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:160:    const users = await db.orm.public.User.all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:166:The rule to remember: inside a request, `db` does everything the `db` from `postgres()` does, so any documented `db.orm...`, `db.sql...`, `db.raw...`, `db.transaction(...)`, `db.prepare(...)` or `db.query(...)` snippet works unchanged. Await every query before the `await using` scope ends. The connection's `close()` waits until the runtime has been idle for one turn of the event loop (a `setTimeout(0)`), then refuses new work. A lazy ORM read such as `db.orm.public.User.all()`, or `db.query(plan)`, returned from the scope without `await` starts only when the caller awaits it, after the scope has closed, so it rejects with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Runtime is closed\"), whose `fix` names the missing `await`. Work that keeps the runtime busy from the close onward completes: a `first()`, `execute()` or `transaction()`, an ORM write and its reload, including `include(...).create()` and a nested `create()`, and an async helper whose steps go through `db.orm`, `db.transaction()` or `db.prepare()`; a transaction commits. Such work is never refused because of the close; if it fails for its own reason, it rejects like any unawaited promise. A helper that calls `db.runtime()` after the scope has ended throws \"Postgres connection is closed\" at that call. Work that starts after the runtime was idle for a turn is refused. Whether a query sent after waiting on something other than the database counts as idle depends on when that callback runs relative to the timer, so do not rely on it. An unawaited promise that fails then, or fails for its own reason, rejects like any unawaited promise, and Node reports it as unhandled. Write `return await db.orm...`, not `return db.orm...`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:173:- **After `db.close()`** (or the end of the `await using` scope), `db.runtime()` throws `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Postgres connection is closed\"), and ORM queries, `db.transaction(...)` and prepared statements that start after the runtime was idle for a turn reject with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Runtime is closed\"). Call `postgres.connect({ url })` again for a new connection.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:364:  const user = await tx.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:365:  await tx.orm.public.Post.create({ userId: user.id, title: 'hello' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:374:  const posts = await tx.orm.public.Post.published().forUpdate().all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:415:`path` is optional at construct time (you can call `db.connect({ path })` later); omit it and the façade still returns a client. The SQLite façade exposes the same `db.sql`, `db.orm`, `db.raw`, `db.prepare(...)`, `db.transaction(...)`, `db.close()`, and `[Symbol.asyncDispose]` surfaces as Postgres, with one addressing difference: SQLite has no schemas, so `db.sql` and `db.orm` are the unbound namespace itself (`db.orm.User`, `db.sql.user`) instead of Postgres's `db.orm.public.User` / `db.sql.public.user`. `db.orm` and `tx.orm` also offer `fragment` beside the models, unless a model is named `fragment`: `const titled = db.orm.fragment({ title: { codecId: 'sqlite/text@1', nullable: false } }, (rows) => rows.where((r) => r.title.eq('Hello')));` then `await db.orm.Post.with(titled).all();`. The Mongo façade shares `db.orm`, `db.close()`, and `[Symbol.asyncDispose]` but has no `db.sql` and no `db.transaction(...)`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:437:8. **Script hangs after queries finish on Postgres.** The `pg.Pool` keeps Node's event loop alive. Solution: `await db.close()` before the script returns, or `await using db = postgres({ contract, ... })` at the top of a script module. Do not put `await using db = postgres(...)` inside a request handler — it's block-scoped and would close the pool after every request. The right server pattern is a module-level singleton in `db.ts` that lives for the process lifetime. On a per-request runtime, use `postgresServerless()` and `await using db = await postgres.connect({ url })` in the handler instead (see *Workflow — Serverless and per-request runtimes*).\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:134:      `createMongoRuntime()` takes `{ context, adapter, driver }`, as the SQL runtime does: create the adapter with `stack.adapter.create(stack)` and pass it beside the context. The runtime reads the target from `context.contract.target`, so build the context over the validated contract. `MongoExecutionContext` no longer has a `stack` member or a second type parameter, and its contract must be a MongoDB contract, as SQL's `ExecutionContext` requires a SQL contract: write `MongoExecutionContext<Contract>`, and build it with `createMongoExecutionContext({ contract, stack })` over a validated contract. `createMongoExecutionStack()` still takes an optional `driver`; given one, it returns `MongoExecutionStackWithDriver`, whose `driver` is always set, and without one it returns `MongoExecutionStack`, which has no `driver` member, as for SQL. `buildMongoEnums()` is removed: read the contract's enum accessors from `db.enums`, from `mongo({ contract })` or `mongoStatic({ contract })`. `MongoExecutionContext` has no `enums` member. The detection matches files that call `createMongoRuntime(`, read `.stack` from a context, give `MongoExecutionContext` two type arguments or a non-contract type argument, or name `buildMongoEnums` or `MongoEnumAccessors`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:145:      `connect()` without options on a `postgres()`, `sqlite()`, `mysql()` or `mongo()` client no longer rejects when the client is connecting or connected. It waits for the connection and resolves with the runtime. `connect(options)` on such a client still rejects with `DRIVER.ALREADY_CONNECTED`, but its message is now `<Target> client is already bound` (for example `Postgres client is already bound`), it has a `why`, and its `fix` changed. Remove code that catches `DRIVER.ALREADY_CONNECTED` from a `connect()` without options, or that avoids calling `connect()` after a query: neither is needed. Update code or tests that match the old message `<Target> client already connected` or the old `fix` \"Call connect() at most once per client.\". A `connect()` that is waiting when `close()` is called now rejects with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:446:Put the descriptor on the execution stack, create the driver from the stack, and connect it to a binding. A `{ kind: 'url', url, dbName }` binding makes the driver open a `MongoClient` and close it on `close()`. A `{ kind: 'mongoClient', client, dbName }` binding uses your client and leaves it open.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:463:const driver = stack.driver.create();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:467:Replace `MongoDriverImpl.fromDb(client.db(dbName))` with `driver.connect({ kind: 'mongoClient', client, dbName })` on a driver created the same way. Code that only sends DDL through a control adapter can use the control driver instead: `await mongoControlDriver.create(url)`, with `mongoControlDriver` the default export of `@prisma/orm-mongo/driver/control`. The control driver uses the database the URL names, so put `dbName` in the URL's path, for example `mongodb://localhost:27017/app`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:469:`createMongoRuntime(...)` closes the driver when the runtime closes, so keep calling `runtime.close()` and drop any separate `driver.close()`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:512:const runtime = createMongoRuntime({ context, adapter: stack.adapter.create(stack), driver });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.16-to-8.0.0-rc.17/instructions.md:640:- const posts = await db.orm.public.Post.apply(notDeleted).apply(postSummary).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.16-to-8.0.0-rc.17/instructions.md:641:+ const posts = await db.orm.public.Post.with(notDeleted).with(postSummary).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.16-to-8.0.0-rc.17/instructions.md:647:- db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.apply(postSummary));\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.16-to-8.0.0-rc.17/instructions.md:648:+ db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.with(postSummary));\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.16-to-8.0.0-rc.17/instructions.md:690:- const postSummary = db.orm.public.Post.scope((posts) => posts.select('id', 'title').include('user'));\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.16-to-8.0.0-rc.17/instructions.md:691:+ const postSummary = db.orm.public.Post.fragment((posts) => posts.select('id', 'title').include('user'));\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.16-to-8.0.0-rc.17/instructions.md:745:A lock cannot be combined with `include`, `groupBy`, `aggregate`, `distinct`, `distinctOn` or a mutation terminal, and a lock method cannot be called inside an `include()` refinement callback. Each of these throws `ORM.LOCK_INCOMPATIBLE`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:158:      A `char(n)` column now reads the same through `.include()` as through a flat read: without the trailing spaces that pad it, where an include used to return them, and keeping a trailing tab or newline, which a flat read used to drop. Compare `char` values without their padding.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:172:      On SQLite, an `.include()` of a row whose `sql/int@1` column holds an INTEGER past 2^53, or a REAL, now throws `RUNTIME.DECODE_FAILED`, where it read the value rounded or with a fraction. Store such values in a `BigInt` or `Float` column.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:666:A `contract.json` with such a default, emitted by an earlier version or edited by hand, still loads. `db init`, `db update` and `migration plan` used to plan the default; they now stop with `CONTRACT.DEFAULT_INVALID`, which names the table, the column, the codec and the value. Emit the contract again with this version, and correct the default in the contract source if emit refuses it. Running a `migration.ts` that an earlier version planned with such a default fails with the same message; correct the default in that file. Every statement that writes a default reads it this way: a new table or column, a changed default, and on SQLite a rebuilt table, which used to write a changed default or a rebuilt table's defaults unread. Each element of a list default is read the same way, and the message names the element's position: `Column \"post\".\"tags\" has a default (element 2) its codec pg/text@1 refuses: pg/text@1 JSON value must be a string`. A NULL element stays NULL. The codecs that carry PostgreSQL's own date and time text, those of `DateString`, `TimeString`, `TimestampString`, `TimestamptzString` and `Timetz`, read only a date or time in ISO 8601 or as PostgreSQL writes it, so such a default as `'now'`, which PostgreSQL would read once when it creates the table, is refused the same way. An `.include()` of such a column reads the text PostgreSQL writes in the ISO DateStyle, its default; on a server set to another DateStyle it now fails with `RUNTIME.DECODE_FAILED`, where it passed the text through. Set `DateStyle` to `ISO` on that server.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:741:`pg/text-array@1`, the codec of a contract-free `textArray()` column, reads a `text[]` column's NULL elements as `null`, so its application type is `readonly (string | null)[]` where it was `readonly string[]`. Code typed by a `textArray()` column, or by `min` or `max` over one, sees `string | null` elements; handle the `null`. Read through an `.include()`, a two-dimensional `text[]` value now throws `RUNTIME.DECODE_FAILED`, where it read as the text `\"a,b\"`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:745:PostgreSQL pads a `char(n)` value with spaces to its length: `'a'` in a `char(3)` column is stored as `'a  '`. A flat read dropped every trailing whitespace character, so a stored `'a\\t'` also read as `\"a\"`, while `.include()` returned the padded text, `\"a  \"`. Both reads now return the value without the padding and nothing more: `\"a\"` for `'a'`, and `\"a\\t\"` for `'a\\t'`. On SQLite, which does not pad, both reads drop trailing spaces, as a flat read did. Code that compared an included `char` value with its padding, or relied on a flat read dropping a trailing tab or newline, compares the value without its padding.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:753:`sql/int@1` holds a JavaScript safe integer. On SQLite, an INTEGER column can hold a larger integer or a REAL. An `.include()` read such a value rounded or with a fraction; it now throws `RUNTIME.DECODE_FAILED`, naming the codec. A flat read is unchanged. Store an integer past 2^53 in a `BigInt` column and a fraction in a `Float` column.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:990:`where`, `orderBy`, `limit`, `offset`, `distinct`, `distinctOn`, `cursor` and `include` now return the collection they were called on, with what they establish added to its type as a fact. A custom collection class keeps its methods through the chain, so `db.Post.where({ userId }).withTitle('orm')` and `db.Post.include('user').withTitle('orm')` compile.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1021:+ const ids = (await db.orm.public.Post.where({ userId }).select('id').limit(10).all()).map((p) => p.id);\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1040:await db.Post.cursor({ id } as never).all(); // error: The 'this' context of type 'PostCollection' is not assignable to method's 'this' of type 'HasOrderBy'\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1046:- await db.Post.cursor({ id } as never).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1047:+ await db.Post.orderBy((post) => post.id.asc()).cursor({ id }).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1056:await unordered.cursor({ id }).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1160:+ const withUser = posts.include('user');\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1173:    .all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.11-to-8.0.0-rc.12/instructions.md:525:  .all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.11-to-8.0.0-rc.12/instructions.md:529:  .all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.17-to-8.0.0-rc.18/instructions.md:571:A state built by hand for an include child must not reuse the parent's table aliases. Prefer building includes through `collection.include(...)` and reading `collection.state`, which aliases the child's tables from the parent's scope. Where a child state is built by hand for a relation whose target table already appears in the parent chain (a self-relation, or an include that returns to an ancestor's table), build it through `include()` instead; `createCollectionTables` starts a fresh scope and would give the child the same alias as its parent.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.17-to-8.0.0-rc.18/instructions.md:616:This affects only an `IncludeExpr` object written out by hand with a `through` descriptor. An include built with `collection.include('<relation>')` carries the aliased junction table already.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.17-to-8.0.0-rc.18/instructions.md:625:const [include] = users.include('tags').state.includes;\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.17-to-8.0.0-rc.18/instructions.md:698:- await db.orm.Post.where({ user_id: 1 }).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.17-to-8.0.0-rc.18/instructions.md:699:+ await db.orm.Post.where({ userId: 1 }).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.17-to-8.0.0-rc.18/instructions.md:819:prepared.all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:21:| Eager-load a reference relation | **ORM `.include('<relation>')`** | Lowers to `$lookup`; composes with `.where` / `.select` / `.orderBy` / `.limit`. |\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:37:const users = await db.orm.users.all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:47:  .all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:59:const articles = await db.orm.posts.variant('article').all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:60:const tutorials = await db.orm.posts.variant('tutorial').where({ authorId }).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:65:**`.first()` vs `.all()`.** `.first()` issues a limit-1 read; `.all()` returns every matching document. There is no `.first({ pk })` shorthand on Mongo — filter on the id field explicitly: `.where({ id }).first()`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:67:Mongo `.all()` returns the same `AsyncIterableResult` shape as Postgres — `await db.orm.users.all()` yields an array; see *Consuming the result* in [`queries.md`](./queries.md).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
